# Line-ending policy.
#
# Several skills parse or hash their example files byte for byte. If Git rewrites
# line endings on checkout (the default on Windows, where core.autocrlf=true), those
# bytes change and the results change with them: an email attachment stored as 7bit
# text gets a different SHA-256, and a fixture's size no longer matches. Pin the
# endings here so a clone behaves identically on Windows, Linux, and macOS.

# Normalize every text file to LF in the repository and check it out as LF.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Shell scripts must be LF or the kernel fails to find the interpreter.
*.sh text eol=lf

# PowerShell is the Windows-side entry point; keep it to the platform convention.
*.ps1 text eol=crlf

# Byte-exact fixtures: never translate these. Email samples carry attachments whose
# hashes are asserted, and the PE and archive samples are binary by nature.
*.eml -text
*.bin -text
*.zip -text
*.gz -text
*.evtx -text
*.pcap -text

# Keep GitHub's language stats focused on the tooling rather than the sample data.
skills/*/examples/** linguist-vendored
